feat('u17-participle-human-nominalization','Human present-participle substantives','new_production','The two complete48-cell -ende systems use ordinary strong/weak adjective agreement. In40.5 hine ondrǣdendum, the participle is dative plural beneficiaries of mercy and hine its accusative object; om.on those rules out adding a demonstrative. In40.7 ðā hyngrendan, weak accusative plural identifies hungry people as the direct object of fill. A dative noun phrase is not an independent dative subject of fear, and hungry participles do not authorize personal finite hyngran.', ['U17G-FB38','U17G-FB40','U17G-FB41','U17G-FB93','U17G-FB100','U17G-BT749'],['40.5','40.7'],['u07-participle-agreement','u13-ondraedan-acc','u14-nominalized-expressions'])
feat('u17-predicate-participle-plural','Predicate participle base and plural-e','new_production','Past predicate participles may be uninflected or have the strong masculine agreement endings regardless of the subject’s gender. The selected37.5 worhte and gesceapene are plural predicate forms, not finite weak preterites. No extra full attributive declension of wyrcean is inferred. Present/past base pairs remain separately bounded.', ['U17G-FB41','U17G-WRIGHT218','U17G-FB190'],['37.5'],['u07-participle-agreement','u07-passive'])
feat('u17-herian-object','Praise a person or entity','new_production','Hęrian takes the accusative praised person/entity. Imperative plural addresses the named beings; present third-plural subjunctive hęrien with hīe expresses let them praise. Indicative/imperative plural hęriað are syncretic, but syntactic roles are distinct.', ['U17G-FB174','U17G-FB175','U17G-FB71','U17G-FB72'],['37.1–5','37.7','37.13'])
feat('u17-giernan-request-woo','Genitive request and wooing','new_production','Giernan governs the desired/requested thing or marriage partner in the genitive. Æt+dative identifies the person asked in39.3. The invariant relative ðe avoids demanding productive relative case attraction; the main-clause eall ðæt retains its own accusative object role.43.14’s genitive maiden is a marriage request, not a direct-object accusative.', ['U17G-BT495','U17G-FB296','U17G-EX68','U17G-FB91'],['39.3','43.14'])
feat('u17-filling-material','Filling and material complements','new_production','Āfyllan takes an accusative entity and mid+dative contents. Its passive has the filled entity as nominative subject. Oferwyrcean38.1 independently takes the covered throne accusative and mid+dative gold. The shared case expression does not collapse the two lexical meanings.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB93'],['38.1','40.1','40.7'])
feat('u17-onfon-person-dative','Accept a person into care','new_production','Dative his cnihte Israel in40.8 extends the receiving frame to a person accepted/protected.39.6 edlēane uses the already taught dative thing received. BT attests accusative receiving elsewhere; an accusative is therefore not globally invalid, but does not instantiate the selected Cook164j dative frame.', ['U17G-FB94','U17G-FB308','U17G-BT750','U17G-BT751'],['39.6','40.8'],['u14-onfon-dative','u15-onfon-payment-acc'])
feat('u17-secgan-predicate','Call a person blessed','new_production','In40.3 future present sęcgað has first-singular accusative mē as object and feminine accusative singular ēadige as its agreeing predicate: generations call Mary blessed. It is not a change to first-person verbal agreement.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB99'],['40.3'])
feat('u17-verb-lexical-extensions','Bounded new senses of familiar verbs','new_production','Explicitly teach dōn fulfil a word37.8, healdan sustain all things41.2, and sęcgan call with an agreeing object predicate40.3. Wyrcean perform the Father’s will41.3 reuses prior work/perform, while building the foundation39.5 uses make/build with its stated local phrase. Do not infer these senses merely from familiar roots.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS'],['37.8','39.5','40.3','41.2–3'])
feat('u17-virtual-relative','Indefinite place and choice clauses','new_production','Cook197 licenses subjunctive clauses effectively conditional/indefinite: choose which one you wish43.12, and have no place where one might lay one’s head42.7. Wille is second-singular present subjunctive of willan; āhylde third-singular present subjunctive. These do not introduce a universal rule that every relative takes subjunctive or that English may requires a modal.', ['U17G-FB105','U17G-EX-PROMPTS'],['42.7','43.12'])
feat('u17-leornian-source','Learning from a person','new_production','Of+dative names the source person in43.11, independently of earlier æt+dative learning. Gewilnian introduces intended-content ðæt; leornie agrees with embedded first-singular ic, while habbað agrees with plural gē.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB104'],['43.11'],['u12-aet-learning-source'])
