feat('u17-temporal-before','Past prospective before','new_production','In38.8 nolde denotes the speaker’s former unwillingness to believe; ǣr ic self hit gesāwe refers to the seeing prospectively from that earlier viewpoint. The first-person past subjunctive must not be misidentified as indicative2sg merely because the spellings match. English self remains despite om.my; the last verb control is retained.', ['U17G-FB105','U17G-EX-PROMPTS'],['38.8'],['u12-reported-sequence'])
feat('u17-negative-concord-review','Negative concord and no contraction inference','previously_taught','41.4 and43.2 retain explicit183 negative-concord control; nān with ne makes one negation.43.8 uses ne witon, not an independently recalled nytan form. The reading’s nāst is supplied as its precise contracted2sg form.', ['U17G-FB100','U17G-FB174'],['41.4','43.2','43.8'],['u15-negative-concord'])
feat('u17-past-tense-selection','Simple preterite and English perfect','previously_taught','Cook188 expressly permits the simple preterite for all three past tenses. Thus37.6 first sętte, and40.4/6–8 simple preterites, can represent the English perfect without inventing new auxiliary-plus-participle cells. Analytic habban perfect is a defensible alternative where the precise participle is actually taught or supplied. An om.hath control reinforces a target; its absence is not a command to calque every English perfect.', ['U17G-FB101','U17G-FB102'],['37.6','40.4','40.6–8'],['u12-preterite-anterior','u12-habban-perfect'])
feat('u17-genitive-reward-cause','Reward for work','new_production','In39.6 his weorces is an adnominal causal genitive with edlēane: reward for his work. The worker receives dative edlēane; æt Gode is the source. Cook153e example lēan ðissa swǣsenda independently supports the causal genitive.', ['U17G-FB90','U17G-FB94'],['39.6'])
sup('U17-H-37-06',['37.6'],'hē sętte bebod and ne gewīt','Second coordinated predicate has the understood decree as subject, as Cook om.it requires; do not retain God as subject of pass. Present gewīt has future reference under188. Supply this whole compressed sequence.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB101','U17G-FB102'])
sup('U17-H-37-14',['37.14'],'horn his folces, lof ealra his hālgena, bearna Israeles, folces him nēalǣcendes','Accusative horn is the exalted strength/power image; lof is appositional praise. Genitives qualify the named people/saints/children; hālgena is weak noun hālga, not adjective hālig. Folces is genitive neuter singular collective; nēalǣcendes agrees with it and him is the dative approached person. Entire dense phrase is supplied, including foreign Israel inflection.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB41','U17G-FB94'])
sup('U17-H-38-09',['38.9'],'Māra is ðīn wīsdōm and ðīn weorc ðonne se hlīsa wǣre ðe ic hīerde','Preserve explicit wǣre as a third-singular past subjunctive referring to the report, not as a finite agreeing with wisdom/work. The singular predicate construes wisdom and its work together; a defensible plural matrix formulation is valid language. The exact printed wǣre is a visible reference position, not an independent new mood-recall test.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB102','U17G-FB104'])
sup('U17-H-41-01',['41.1'],'ǣfre wæs his wīsdōm of him ācęnned','Whole theological source relation: wisdom is begotten of him. Of him is not a general newly taught passive agent marker; passive morphology alone does not teach that relation.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB101'])
sup('U17-H-41-05',['41.5'],'ēow bið seald; ēow bið geopenod','Impersonal/neuter-singular passive giving and opening, with dative addressees. English it shall omitted at first target; finite bið is the simple be auxiliary with future reference. No independent accusative object is invented.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB101','U17G-FB102'])
sup('U17-H-42-01',['42.1'],'Ðæs geongan mannes lufu befēoll on ðæt mǣden','Love is the grammatical subject; the young man is genitive possessor, and the girl is on+accusative target. Ex42.1 supplies mīn lufu befielð on ðē; adapt its person/tense using fēoll from109. Original175.20 has gefēoll; preserve the exercise’s explicit be-lexeme in the model. No complete befeallan finite system is assigned.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB66','U17G-FB175'])
sup('U17-H-42-02-PART',['42.2'],'ðāra hearpera stefne mid hiera hearpum hearpiendra','Stefne is the heard accusative voice. Hearpera and hearpiendra are genitive plural identifying the performers; their own harps are dative after mid. The following hīe refers to harpers, not singular voice. Supply whole nested phrase.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB41','U17G-FB99'])
sup('U17-H-42-03',['42.3'],'swā micle męnigu swā nān mann gerīman ne mæg, of eallum ðēodum and of ǣlcre mǣgðe, standende beforan Godes ðrymsetle, ealle mid hwītum gegierelum gescrȳdde and palmtwīg on hiera handum healdende','Męnigu is grammatically accusative feminine singular company; subsequent ealle, clothing and their hands refer by sense to its members. Standende and healdende are predicate participles accompanying perceived people, not progressive finite verbs. Gescrȳdde is the plural predicate past participle. Preserve collective singular versus semantic plural distinction; whole phrase is supplied rather than teaching unrestricted shifts of agreement.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB41','U17G-FB102'])
sup('U17-H-42-06',['42.6'],'Ūrum Gode sīe blētsung and beorhtnes, wīsdōm and ðancung, weorðscipe and stręngðu, ā ā ā','Dative recipient of a singular optative attribution; coordinated abstract praise formulas are supplied whole. This does not create a universal singular-agreement rule for coordinated subjects.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB102','U17G-EX48'])
sup('U17-H-43-01',['43.1'],'Ǣlc flǣsc is gærs, and ðæs flǣsces wuldor is swilce ðāra wyrta blōstma','Metaphorical flesh/grass and glory/flower comparison; swilce introduces an elliptical comparison, not an independently formed counterfactual clause. Supply the whole figure, with ordinary nominal morphology independently available.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS'])
sup('U17-H-43-03',['43.3'],'swā-swā bieman','Trumpet as comparison standard in accusative, as if lifting a trumpet. Exact printed swā-swā is a supplied phrase reference; the intended elliptical comparison does not assert a physical trumpet is the direct object of the actual command.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS'])
sup('U17-H-43-16',['43.16'],'ðæt hine man slōge','Indefinite man is grammatical subject one/someone; hine refers to the man who had the expectation. Past subjunctive slōge expresses the contemplated slaying, without asserting that death occurred. The active paraphrase is expressly printed in the prompt.', ['U17G-EX-PROMPTS','U17G-FB104'])
